        "answer": {
          "short": "For $x=0,1,2,3,4$: $0.25,\\ 0.50,\\ 0.125,\\ 0.10,\\ 0.025$.",
          "worked": "Read each bar height at $x=0,1,2,3,4$, respectively: $P(X=x)=0.25,\\ 0.50,\\ 0.125,\\ 0.10,\\ 0.025$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 0,
        "responseSpace": "scaffold"
      },
      {
        "id": "p7-q3-f",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "Show the sum of probabilities is 1. How does this relate to the histogram area?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$0.25+0.50+0.125+0.10+0.025=1$. Each bar has width 1, so its area equals its probability; the total area is 1.",
          "worked": "$$0.25+0.50+0.125+0.10+0.025=1.$$For each rectangle, area $=\\text{width}\\times\\text{height}=1\\times\\text{relative frequency}=\\text{probability}$. Hence the total histogram area is the sum of the probabilities, 1.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 19
      },
      {
        "id": "p7-q3-g",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "g",
        "prompt": "Explain why the area under the relative frequency polygon equals the area of the histogram.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The triangular areas removed from the tops of bars balance the triangular areas added between them, including the end triangles, so the total area stays 1.",
          "worked": "Between adjacent bar centres the polygon forms a trapezium of width 1, with area equal to half the sum of the two bar heights. The end triangles each contribute half the first or last bar height. Altogether each bar height is counted once, giving the same total area as the unit-width histogram.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 26
      },
      {
        "id": "p7-q3-h",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "h",
        "prompt": "Show the mean number of cars per household is 1.15.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$E(X)=1.15$ cars per household.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{aligned}E(X)&=0(0.25)+1(0.50)+2(0.125)+3(0.10)+4(0.025)\\\\&=0+0.50+0.25+0.30+0.10\\\\&=1.15.\\end{aligned}$$The population average is 1.15 cars per household.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      },
      {
        "id": "p8-i",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "i",
        "prompt": "For a street of 100 households, how many cars in total would you expect? Are the assumptions reasonable?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "115 cars, assuming the street has the suburb’s car-ownership distribution. This may be unreasonable for some streets.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{Expected total cars}&=100\\times1.15\\\\&=115.\\end{aligned}$$This assumes the street’s car-ownership distribution matches the suburb’s. Streets near train stations or in wealthier areas may differ, so the assumption may not hold.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
